5.1 Append-only protection

Implement a PostgreSQL "BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE" trigger on "governance.audit_logs" that rejects mutation.

The application-level audit service must expose only the operations needed for validated insertion and authorized querying. Do not provide ordinary update or delete methods.

The migration may replace its own trigger/function definitions where necessary, but it must not drop or recreate the audit table to bypass protection.

Document the threat boundary: a database owner or sufficiently privileged administrator may be able to disable a trigger. Mitigate this by using least-privilege runtime database credentials, restricting schema ownership and DDL privileges, and keeping production migration privileges separate from ordinary application permissions where the deployment supports that separation.

Test direct SQL "UPDATE" and "DELETE" attempts against the trigger. Also verify that ordinary audit insertion and querying continue to work.

Do not promise cryptographic tamper-proofing or protection against a database superuser unless an independently verified mechanism provides it.

5.2 Event identifiers and indexes

Require unique event identifiers and use deterministic idempotency where retries could otherwise create duplicate records.

Do not make ordinary business retries generate duplicate audit events. The idempotency design must not silently merge distinct real actions.

Add indexes supporting timestamp filtering, action filtering, entity history, actor history, and request-ID lookup. Avoid indexes that do not support a demonstrated query or acceptance requirement.

